From: Neal Cardwell <ncardwell@google.com>
Subject: [EXPERIMENT 1] tcp: detect app-limited gaps before ACK and timer handling

Upstream-Repository: https://github.com/google/bbr.git
Upstream-Commit: 137a508c950878712068b1d7a67ece75e4f2835c
Ubuntu-Base: d974a4063f5c03c13b4f241a9ab511750e0b9f12

Port the two upstream call sites to Ubuntu 7.0.0-30.30. The timer
context no longer has an empty line before tcp_mstamp_refresh; use the
actual Ubuntu context without changing call ordering. The ACK call
remains immediately after the prior_in_flight snapshot.

This is one prerequisite, not a complete BBRv3 port. Original authorship
and the surrounding Linux source license are retained.

diff --git a/net/ipv4/tcp_input.c b/net/ipv4/tcp_input.c
--- a/net/ipv4/tcp_input.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/tcp_input.c
@@ -4311,6 +4311,7 @@
 
 	prior_fack = tcp_is_sack(tp) ? tcp_highest_sack_seq(tp) : tp->snd_una;
 	rs.prior_in_flight = tcp_packets_in_flight(tp);
+	tcp_rate_check_app_limited(sk);
 
 	/* ts_recent update must be made after we are sure that the packet
 	 * is in window.
diff --git a/net/ipv4/tcp_timer.c b/net/ipv4/tcp_timer.c
--- a/net/ipv4/tcp_timer.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/tcp_timer.c
@@ -706,6 +706,7 @@
 			       tcp_timeout_expires(sk));
 		return;
 	}
+	tcp_rate_check_app_limited(sk);
 	tcp_mstamp_refresh(tcp_sk(sk));
 	event = icsk->icsk_pending;
 
